---
/**
 * Protocol — the slice lifecycle (pending → coded → reviewed → done, with the
 * blocked → rework return path) as an inline diagram with a staged,
 * scroll-triggered reveal. This carries the site's ONE permitted script
 * (ADR-0002).
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Protocol (ADR-0003: the copy
 * deck is the single source of truth). Every copy string sits on ONE source
 * line and is never split by an inline element, so the built output greps
 * literally against the deck (convention from phase-1-ws-1.1, documented in
 * phase-1-ws-1.2). Two consequences worth naming:
 *
 *   - The deck's captions already begin with the state name ("pending — …"),
 *     so the states carry no separate heading element: one would duplicate the
 *     word, and wrapping the name in a <span> to style it would put markup
 *     inside the string. The state name loses distinct typography; a literal
 *     cross-vendor grep is worth more than that.
 *   - `metis next` is backticked in the deck but renders as plain text here,
 *     for the same reason — a <code> element inside the caption would break
 *     the grep.
 *
 * Structure is the diagram: <ol> carries the order, so the flow reads
 * correctly with CSS disabled and the connectors between states can be purely
 * decorative CSS. The "blocked → rework" arrow is different — that string IS
 * rendered copy, so its "→" is a real character in the markup rather than a
 * CSS ::before, exactly as phase-1-ws-1.2 treats its em dashes.
 *
 * Color: aegean is decorative-only in here. #2E8C83 on the card surface
 * #171B22 is 4.27:1 — below AA for text, and a pairing tokens.css's ratio
 * table does not cover (it documents aegean on the base #0E1116 at 4.68:1).
 * So aegean draws connectors and the loop chip's boundary, which carry no
 * text and no information the markup lacks; all diagram text uses
 * --color-text / --color-muted, whose pairings are in that table.
 *
 * Animation (ADR-0002): the base rules render the finished diagram. The
 * staged reveal lives entirely inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`
 * and is keyed off an `is-revealed` class, with `animation-fill-mode:
 * backwards` holding each state's start frame through its stagger delay —
 * that is what avoids an `opacity: 0` base. With JS off, the observer
 * unsupported, or reduced motion set, the diagram is complete and static,
 * never hidden (ADR-0005's never-invisible rule, ahead of its slice).
 *
 * The script at the foot of this file is the one the site is allowed
 * (ADR-0002). `is:inline` is required: a processed script element would be
 * bundled into an external module, and the policy is an *inline* observer.
 * is:inline is passed through verbatim and is not minified, so it is written
 * hand-minified on one line.
 *
 * It watches the section rather than the diagram: intersectionRatio is a
 * fraction of the target's own height, so a threshold on a diagram taller
 * than the viewport could never be met on a narrow phone and the reveal would
 * never fire. Watching the section at the default threshold also arms the
 * stagger at the heading, before the states scroll up into view. The class
 * lands on an element declared in this component, which is what lets Astro's
 * scoped selectors match it. Unobserves on first hit — the reveal plays once.
 *
 * This rationale lives in the frontmatter, not in an HTML comment beside the
 * script: frontmatter comments are compiled away, and an HTML comment naming
 * a script element literally would survive into dist and break the phase
 * gate's "exactly one script element in dist/index.html" count.
 */
---

<section id="protocol" class="section">
  <h2>One slice at a time. Every time.</h2>

  <ol class="lifecycle">
    <li class="state">pending — dispatched by metis next, never chosen by the agent</li>
    <li class="state">coded — brief committed first, verify green, scope audited</li>
    <li class="state">reviewed — a different agent signs off, identity checked</li>
    <li class="state">done — archived with the full paper trail</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="loop">blocked → rework</p>

  <p class="closing">Findings become rules. Rules prevent repeats. The system gets better because it remembers.</p>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* --- The flow ----------------------------------------------------------
     Mobile-first: a single column, chained top to bottom. The four-across
     row is one media query at 1024px — below that the captions would be
     ~28 characters wide and the "diagram" would read as four tall slivers.
     A two-column intermediate step is deliberately skipped: it would wrap
     the chain mid-flow and the connectors would have to change direction
     between items 2 and 3. */
  .lifecycle {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    /* Drops the UA list indent — the token zero, not a raw 0 (f-003). */
    padding: var(--space-0);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Text on surface: 16.14:1 dark, 18.91:1 light (tokens.css ratio table).
     The hairline only sharpens an edge the fill already draws, so the
     decorative --color-border is the right token, as in Personas. */
  .state {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* --- Connectors --------------------------------------------------------
     Decorative: the <ol> already carries order and direction, so nothing is
     lost when these are unsupported, unstyled, or unseen. ::before is the
     line, ::after the head; the line's length matches the grid gap exactly,
     so it spans the space between two cards and touches neither. */
  .state:not(:first-child)::before,
  .state:not(:first-child)::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    background-color: var(--color-secondary);
  }

  .state:not(:first-child)::before {
    inset-block-end: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    width: 2px;
    height: var(--space-4);
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  /* Two borders on a rotated square make the chevron: 135deg points it down
     the column. translate(-50%, 50%) centres it on the card's top edge, at
     the end of the line above. */
  .state:not(:first-child)::after {
    inset-block-end: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    width: var(--space-2);
    height: var(--space-2);
    border-block-start: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    border-inline-end: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    background-color: transparent;
    transform: translate(-50%, 50%) rotate(135deg);
  }

  /* 1024px is a raw length by design: tokens.css owns colour, type and
     spacing and deliberately defines no breakpoint scale (the reasoning
     phase-1-ws-1.2 recorded for its 768px query). */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .lifecycle {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    /* The chain turns to run left-to-right: the line moves into the gap
       before each card, the chevron rotates to point along it. */
    .state:not(:first-child)::before {
      inset-block-end: auto;
      inset-block-start: 50%;
      inset-inline-start: auto;
      inset-inline-end: 100%;
      width: var(--space-5);
      height: 2px;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
    }

    .state:not(:first-child)::after {
      inset-block-end: auto;
      inset-block-start: 50%;
      inset-inline-start: auto;
      inset-inline-end: 100%;
      transform: translate(50%, -50%) rotate(45deg);
    }
  }

  /* --- The block path ----------------------------------------------------
     A chip hanging off the flow on a dashed riser. Its dashed boundary is
     decorative — the label is the information — and aegean on the page base
     is 4.68:1 either way, past the 3:1 that a meaningful boundary would
     need. Muted text on base: 8.39:1 dark, 5.66:1 light. */
  .loop {
    position: relative;
    /* Leaves exactly the riser's height between the flow and the chip. */
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    /* fit-content over the inherited --measure: the chip should hug its
       label rather than stretch to a paragraph's width. Centred so the riser
       meets the flow where the block path actually leaves it: in the column
       layout that is the gap between "coded" and "reviewed" — the centre of a
       four-track grid falls exactly there — and in the stacked layout it is
       the chain's own vertical line, which runs down the middle of the
       cards. Left-aligned, the chip would appear to hang off "pending". */
    width: fit-content;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border: 1px dashed var(--color-secondary);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The riser. Half the 2px stroke back from centre, so it lands on the
     midpoint rather than 2px past it. */
  .loop::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-end: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: calc(50% - 1px);
    width: var(--space-0);
    height: var(--space-5);
    border-inline-start: 2px dashed var(--color-secondary);
  }

  .closing {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- Staged reveal ------------------------------------------------------
     Opt-in only. Everything above renders the finished diagram, so no-JS,
     unsupported-observer and reduced-motion users all get the complete final
     state with no delay — the `is-revealed` class adds motion, it never adds
     visibility. `backwards` fill makes each state hold the start frame
     through its delay, so the stagger needs no hidden base state.
     320ms each, longest delay 560ms: inside ADR-0002's 200-700ms envelope,
     and nothing loops. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .is-revealed .state,
    .is-revealed .loop {
      animation: state-in 320ms var(--ease-out) backwards;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(2) {
      animation-delay: 140ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(3) {
      animation-delay: 280ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(4) {
      animation-delay: 420ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .loop {
      animation-delay: 560ms;
    }
  }

  @keyframes state-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(var(--space-2));
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script is:inline>new IntersectionObserver((es,o)=>es.forEach(e=>{if(e.isIntersecting){e.target.classList.add("is-revealed");o.unobserve(e.target)}})).observe(document.getElementById("protocol"))</script>
